WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.477
Specs and acceptance criteria that an agent can check.

2
00:00:03.757 --> 00:00:07.768
A spec for an agent is a short document, not a requirements tome.

3
00:00:08.048 --> 00:00:09.776
Four parts do most of the work.

4
00:00:10.056 --> 00:00:10.803
Goal.

5
00:00:11.083 --> 00:00:14.027
One or two sentences on the outcome and who it’s for.

6
00:00:14.307 --> 00:00:19.555
“Customers with expired cards can update their card at checkout without losing their cart.

7
00:00:19.835 --> 00:00:20.132
”.

8
00:00:20.412 --> 00:00:21.863
Acceptance criteria.

9
00:00:22.143 --> 00:00:24.127
Specific, observable, checkable.

10
00:00:24.407 --> 00:00:27.607
Each should translate directly into a test or a command.

11
00:00:27.887 --> 00:00:47.450
Good criteria name inputs and outputs (“a 101st request within 60 seconds gets HTTP 429 with a Retry-After header”), edge cases the agent can’t guess (“the health checker is never limited”), and the check that defines done (“the new test file and the existing suite pass”).

12
00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:48.690
Non-goals.

13
00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:55.711
What not to do: no new dependencies, no change to the public API, no refactor of the payments module.

14
00:00:55.991 --> 00:01:00.450
Non-goals prevent the scope creep that shows up later as an unreviewable diff.

15
00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:07.087
They’re also how you tell the agent where your ambition ends, the counterpart to the docs’ “be ambitious” phrase.

16
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:08.903
Constraints and context.

17
00:01:09.183 --> 00:01:19.764
The things it can’t see: a decision from a meeting, a performance budget, a file that must not be touched, where similar code lives (“follow the pattern in HotDogWidget.

18
00:01:20.044 --> 00:01:22.156
php ”, in the docs’ example).

19
00:01:22.436 --> 00:01:23.972
The spec does double duty.

20
00:01:24.252 --> 00:01:27.132
It guides the build, and it’s the yardstick for review.

21
00:01:27.412 --> 00:01:42.794
The best-practices page’s suggested review prompt has a subagent check the diff against the spec, confirm every requirement is implemented and the listed edge cases have tests, and report only gaps that affect correctness, explicitly skipping style,

22
00:01:43.074 --> 00:01:46.786
because a reviewer asked to find problems will always find something.
